WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.799
Thanks to KiwiCo for supporting this episode
of Journey to the Microcosmos!

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:09.950
Click the link in the description to learn
more and for a special offer.

00:00:17.511 --> 00:00:22.520
There have been a lot of significant moments
in evolutionary history.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:28.320
There’s the emergence of unicellular life,
the endosymbiotic origin of chloroplasts and

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:35.000
mitochondria, the transition from life in
water to life on land, and all the mass extinctions

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.000
along the way.

00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:46.710
More than 600 million years ago, one particular
group of single-celled eukaryotes faced their

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.170
own very important fork in the road.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:54.170
Today, we call that group and its descendents
the Choanozoa.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:01:02.200
But at that point in time, they were just
some single cells staring down two futures.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.570
In one future, they would continue life as
single cells.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:11.460
In the other, they would become something
entirely different, something that we have

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.170
to be extremely grateful for.

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:20.320
Because down that other fork came the metazoans,
more commonly known as animals.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.240
So...us.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.950
For the Choanozoa that went down that route,
cells would divide and develop and bind together,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:34.820
creating biological links and relationships
that we see as a whole animal instead of just

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.409
individual cellular selves.

00:01:37.409 --> 00:01:42.590
And while we could spend this episode marveling
at the complexity of animal life, you might

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:48.530
also be wondering…what about that first
group of eukaryotes, the ones who didn’t

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.030
follow the animal path?

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:51.450
What happened to them?

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.830
What do they even look like?

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.170
Well, they look kind of like this.

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.290
No, not the big gold things, those are synura.

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:05.680
And not the big clear thing in the middle
either, that’s an empty frustule of a diatom.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:12.610
No, we’re talking about that small, balloon-looking
thing hanging out on top of the diatom.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.120
So, what is that?

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:20.910
It is a choanoflagellate, but you can call
it a sibling.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:26.660
A very, very, very distant sibling of sorts,
but a very important one.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:32.810
Found in both marine and freshwater, we’ve
only identified around 150 extant species

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.510
of choanoflagellates.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:42.030
But this single-celled flagellate is the closest
ancestor that we know of to animals.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:46.500
The word "choano" translates to “funnel”
in Ancient Greek, a reference to the collar

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.460
that wraps around one end of the choanoflagellate.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:55.530
That collar is made up of microvilli, a series
of thin projections.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.690
But under most microbes, it’s difficult
to make out the individual microvilli, and

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.000
instead they blend together into one big cone.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.550
Encased in that collar is the organism’s
single flagella, which it uses to create currents

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:15.790
in the water to shake up and draw in their
favorite food: bacteria.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:20.810
Some choanoflagellates are non-motile, meaning
they spend most of their life not moving around.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:27.630
Instead, they just stick themselves to a nice,
sturdy surface and grab whatever food passes by.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:32.900
There are also motile choanoflagellates, which
move around with their flagella, but in kind

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.080
of a weird way.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:40.051
You see, other flagellates—like this euglena—use
the twisting rotor movement of their flagella

00:03:40.051 --> 00:03:42.530
to pull them around the microcosmos.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:48.390
But choanoflagellates use their flagellum
to push themselves through the water.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.640
All of these things make choanoflagellates
interesting to watch, but if you’re looking

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:59.400
at them and wondering how they could possibly
be the closest relatives to animals, we don’t

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:00.459
blame you.

00:04:00.459 --> 00:04:06.040
So what made biologists look at them and see
any kind of kinship to animals?

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.150
Why not some other protist?

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:12.970
To see the resemblance, you have to go to
something a bit more fundamental when it comes

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:15.790
to animals: the sponge.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:21.340
Yes, the sponges you find in the ocean (and
also occasionally in freshwater) are animals,

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.419
very primitive ones.

00:04:23.419 --> 00:04:30.120
And in 1867, when naturalist Henry James-Clarke
published an article documenting the choanoflagellates,

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.070
he did more than just describe their appearance.

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:39.570
He compared them to the flagellated cells
that live inside the chamber of sponges, a

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:47.560
resemblance that would be further emphasized
when those sponge cells were named choanocytes.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.780
James-Clarke wrote of their similarities in
a paper titled “Conclusive proofs of the

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:59.780
animality of the ciliate sponges, and of their
affinities with the Infusoria flagellata”.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:05.500
And we have to say that “conclusive proofs”
is a bold statement coming from a biologist,

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:10.190
especially given the number of occasions during
our journey through the microcosmos where

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:15.880
we have seen morphological similarities between
organisms suggesting a close kinship that

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:19.430
genetic research eventually undermines.

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:24.470
But on the other hand, maybe we have a bit
of bias towards those kinds of stories because

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:29.220
a shift in understanding is part of what makes
a story compelling.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:34.960
But what makes James-Clarkes’ observation
fascinating is that a century later, his observations

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.820
and conclusions would be supported by electron
microscopy—which deepened our understanding

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:45.500
of the structures shared by choanoflagellates
and choanocytes.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:51.360
And those observations would later be validated
by molecular phylogeny, which not only established

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:59.259
the genetic resemblance, but named choanoflagellates
as the nearest relative to animals.

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:05.509
Knowing this fact about choanoflagellates
does more than just illuminate our evolutionary

00:06:05.509 --> 00:06:12.080
lineage, it helps shed light on what hundreds
of millions of years of evolution might have

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:13.310
looked like.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:18.699
From the differences and similarities that
exist between choanoflagellates and sponges,

00:06:18.699 --> 00:06:25.199
we can start to make predictions about our
last common ancestor, that unknown organism

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:30.390
that came before the fork in the evolutionary
road.

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:35.800
What we have in common suggests traits that
may have existed in that ancestor, like the

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:42.210
flagellum and collar that we see in both the
choanoflagellate and the sponge choanocyte.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:46.949
And the similarities go beyond the morphological.

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:53.050
While investigating the choanoflagellate genome,
scientists found a number of genes encoding

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.729
proteins that help cells stick to each other.

00:06:56.729 --> 00:07:02.289
These particular proteins were thought to
be an animal thing, acting like glue that

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:04.449
keeps our cells bound together.

00:07:04.449 --> 00:07:09.330
So it’s not completely clear why choanoflagellates
have this molecule.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:14.699
But the fact that they do—along with the
observation that some species group together

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:22.050
into colonies—suggests that whatever our
mysterious shared ancestor was, it was likely

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.039
capable of some basic form of multicellularity.

00:07:26.039 --> 00:07:28.409
Metazoans just ran with it.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:38.280
And of course, the work continues, with scientists
looking to choanoflagellates for whatever

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:42.600
kinship can give us insight into our past.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.289
This is why we look to family and genealogy,
isn’t it?

00:07:46.289 --> 00:07:52.330
We go to our past, to our ancestry, to understand
more about ourselves.

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:58.060
We seek answers in our family resemblances,
and find questions in the differences.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:05.030
And so, while choanoflagellates may seem an
unlikely sibling, they are also, it seems,

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:06.679
a generous one.

00:08:12.889 --> 00:08:19.170
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

